    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to set up some reorder logic for our electronics parts. We're seeing some issues with lithium-ion battery cell stockouts when we hit peak season. Can you help me draft a formula or some basic rules for calculating safety stock levels, specifically considering lead times from our Shenzhen suppliers?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! A basic approach would be to use the formula:\n\n\nSafety Stock Level = (Maximum Daily Usage * Maximum Lead Time) - (Safety Stock Factor * Average Daily Usage)\n\n\nThis formula considers the maximum daily usage rate of the batteries, the maximum lead time from the supplier, and a factor that represents the level of safety stock you want to maintain, usually a percentage of average daily usage.\n\n\nRegarding lead times, let's assume that your maximum lead time for shipments from your Shenzhen suppliers is around 14 days and your maximum daily usage is 500 units. If we want to maintain a 95% service level (Safety Stock Factor), and your average daily usage is 300 units, you'd calculate it as follows:\n\n\nSafety Stock Level = (500 * 14) - (0.95 * 300) = 7000 - 285 = 6715 units\n\n\nTherefore, you should aim to have 6715 units of lithium-ion battery cells in stock to cover the peak season without encountering a stockout. Adjust the Safety Stock Factor according to your desired service level.",
