      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense, but the math seems a bit high for just a buffer. my main concern is the unpredictability of the shipments. sometimes the shipments arrive on time, but other times there are these random delays where parts just seem to vanish or stay stuck in customs for no reason, and it messes up the whole flow. how can i account for those kinds of unexpected disruptions in the logic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I recommend incorporating a disruption risk factor into the safety stock calculation to account for unexpected delays. This factor will increase the safety stock level based on historical data about delays and disruptions.\n\n\nLet's calculate it using an amended formula:\n\nSafety Stock Level = (Maximum Daily Usage * Maximum Lead Time) - (Safety Stock Factor * Average Daily Usage) + Disruption Risk Factor\n\n\nFirst, collect data on past delays and disruptions during the shipments. Calculate the average delay time experienced and the proportion of on-time deliveries. Use this data to establish a Disruption Risk Factor, expressed as a percentage of additional stock. For example, if the average delay is 5 days and you experience delays 15% of the time, your Disruption Risk Factor might be 5 * 15% = 0.75 days.\n\nThen, apply your Safety Stock Factor (e.g., 95%) to the Average Daily Usage (e.g., 300 units).\n\nSo the Safety Stock Level would be:\n\nSafety Stock Level = (500 * 14) + (300 * 14 * 0.95) + (300 * 14 * 0.75)\n\n= 7000 + 3990 + 3150\n\n= 14140 units\n\n\nTherefore,",
